# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE, NOT A WORKFLOW. GitHub does not read it: it lives under docs/ and has no `.yml` name, so nothing in this
# repository can start it. It is the reviewed reference copy (Issue #71, Phase 2 of the verifier repair) and STAYS in the repository
# when the temporary workflow made from it is removed.
#
# THE ACTIVATION COPY (Phase 4 of Issue #71). Everything below the header, comments aside, is copied verbatim into
# .github/workflows/ios-der-entitlements-diagnostics.yml by a reviewed pull request; tool/test_ios_der_diagnostics.sh fails if the two
# differ in anything but comments. Activating it needs ALL of the following, and each RUN needs its own:
#   1. a separate, explicit authorization (the CTO's written decision) to run the experiment, naming that one run;
#   2. the reviewed activation pull request, merged into the default branch (GitHub starts a workflow_dispatch workflow only from a
#      file that exists there), after a fresh review of this file, tool/probe_der_entitlements.sh and tool/der_entitlements_probe.py;
#   3. only then may anyone with write access start it by hand (workflow_dispatch). A workflow file in .github/workflows/ can be
#      started without any gate, which is why the activation copy is temporary: one authorized run, then a reviewed pull request
#      removes it (docs/IOS_SIGNING.md, section 3.1c).
#
# iOS DER ENTITLEMENTS DIAGNOSTICS (Issue #71, Phase 2 of the verifier repair). PREPARED FOR REVIEW; NOT TO BE STARTED
# WITHOUT A SEPARATE, EXPLICIT AUTHORIZATION. It DOES perform signing, of one narrow kind: AD-HOC signing (Apple's own `codesign -s -`) of
# DISPOSABLE SYNTHETIC executables that it builds itself. It is not an application build, not a production signing operation,
# not an Apple Distribution signing operation, not a release and not an upload, and it accesses no Apple signing credential.
#
# Question: what does a DER entitlements blob written by Apple's own `codesign --generate-entitlement-der` really look like?
# tool/ipa_inspect.py parse_der_entitlements assumes a layout (the dictionary as an ASN.1 SET) from public descriptions. On the
# first Apple-signed IPA (Authorization C, run 44834801248) it refused Apple's real blob ("unsupported DER entitlements version
# or layout") and that blob was not kept, so the layout has never been seen on real Apple data. A macOS runner is the only place
# where Apple's tool can be run, and no repository tool can write the answer for it.
#
# What it does, on a GitHub-hosted macos-15 runner with Xcode 26.3 (the pins of the other iOS workflows), by running
# tool/probe_der_entitlements.sh:
#   1. compiles a tiny DISPOSABLE C program (it does nothing) for iOS arm64 and for macOS;
#   2. signs copies of it AD HOC (`codesign -s -`: no identity, no keychain, no timestamp server) with explicit XML
#      entitlements and --generate-entitlement-der: the four keys of the real app, then every value type, in up to three variants:
#      PLAIN; XCODELIKE, a CONTROLLED variant of the flags of the codesign call Xcode made for the real app (identifier set with -i,
#      the certificate-dependent designated requirement removed; it is NOT the command Xcode ran and does not reproduce Xcode's
#      Distribution process); and LOGREF, the Packaging.log call itself with substituted team and certificate-name values, EXPLORATORY
#      and never required. The three REQUIRED cases are runner-ios-xcodelike, runner-mac-plain and arrays-ios-xcodelike;
#      the other 29 are supporting or exploratory (docs/IOS_SIGNING.md, section 3.1b);
#   3. prints Apple's own `codesign` verdict and display of each, and with tool/der_entitlements_probe.py the file identity, the
#      CodeDirectory facts and special-slot seals, the DER blob (size, digest, bytes in hex) and an ASN.1 outline that assumes no
#      layout, as text in the job log; the whole signed file (base64, at most 64 KiB) only for three chosen cases;
#   4. tells apart, for every case, that signing succeeded, that Apple's `codesign --verify` succeeded, that DER evidence was
#      extracted (readable Mach-O, signed, DER and XML blobs present, a valid container with ONE ASN.1 element, both blobs sealed
#      by the CodeDirectory) and whether the CURRENT parser accepts the DER (reported, never required: it is the subject of the
#      investigation), and, for the three required cases, whether the complete signed executable was emitted as a fixture (decoded and
#      checked for size and SHA-256). The step FAILS unless every required case produced DER evidence AND its fixture dump, so a run in which `codesign` "succeeded"
#      but wrote nothing usable is a failed run, not a successful one.
#
# What it must never do, and this workflow is kept to it (tool/test_ios_der_diagnostics.sh checks the files):
#   - use a certificate, provisioning profile, App Store Connect key, password or any stored credential, or sign anything but
#     the disposable program; touch the application, an archive or an IPA;
#   - read the repository's secret store, select an environment, or use the OIDC token;
#   - contact or authenticate to any Apple service; upload anything (there is no artifact upload: the evidence is the log, which in
#     this PUBLIC repository is publicly readable; tool/extract_probe_fixtures.py rebuilds the three fixtures from a saved log; base64
#     is printed only for the disposable synthetic program in the three required cases, a narrow exception to the logging rule of
#     docs/IOS_SIGNING.md section 7, see its section 3.1b);
#   - run on a push, a pull request or a schedule: it is started by hand (workflow_dispatch) only, and has no inputs.
#
# SUCCESS HERE DOES NOT PROVE APPLE DISTRIBUTION COMPATIBILITY. An ad-hoc signature written by Apple's tool is genuine evidence of
# the format Apple's codesign writes. The Packaging.log of run 44834801248 shows that Xcode's own local call for every item, the
# main app included, is an ad-hoc call (`codesign -f -s - ... --generate-entitlement-der`), that the cloud service receives only the
# CodeDirectory digest, and that its CMS signature is attached afterwards (`--edit-cms`): the blob of a cloud-signed IPA is written
# by a call of the kind made here. That is strong evidence of its layout, not a proof about that IPA. See docs/IOS_SIGNING.md.

name: iOS DER entitlements diagnostics (credential-free, not a release)

on:
  workflow_dispatch:

# Least privilege: the job only reads this repository. No write access of any kind, no token for other services.
permissions:
  contents: read

concurrency:
  group: ios-der-entitlements-diagnostics
  cancel-in-progress: false

env:
  # The pins of ios-ci-validation.yml and build-variants.yml (tool/verify_ios_toolchain.sh compares those two files only).
  XCODE_APP: /Applications/Xcode_26.3.app
  EXPECTED_XCODE: "26.3"

jobs:
  probe:
    name: Ad-hoc sign a disposable program with Apple's codesign and read the DER entitlements
    # macos-15 is the arm64 image. Not macos-latest.
    runs-on: macos-15
    timeout-minutes: 20
    steps:
      - name: Refuse anything but the first attempt of a manual run on the default branch
        # Only the runner's own variables are read; nothing from the event or the repository reaches the shell. This does not make a
        # modified copy of the file on another branch safe (such a copy has no guard): it stops the reviewed file being run by
        # accident from a feature branch or re-run from the Actions page, and it records WHICH commit ran, so that the commit can be
        # compared with the one the authorization names.
        run: |
          echo "commit $GITHUB_SHA, run $GITHUB_RUN_ID, attempt $GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT, event $GITHUB_EVENT_NAME"
          [ "$GITHUB_EVENT_NAME" = "workflow_dispatch" ] || { echo "::error::Only a manual start is allowed."; exit 1; }
          [ "$GITHUB_REF" = "refs/heads/development" ] || { echo "::error::Only the development branch may run this workflow."; exit 1; }
          [ "$GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT" = "1" ] || { echo "::error::A re-run is not authorized: start a new run only with a new written authorization."; exit 1; }

      - name: Checkout this revision (the repository's own tools)
        uses: actions/checkout@11d5960a326750d5838078e36cf38b85af677262 # v4
        with:
          persist-credentials: false

      - name: Select Xcode 26.3
        run: |
          sudo xcode-select -s "$XCODE_APP/Contents/Developer"
          xcodebuild -version
          actual="$(xcodebuild -version | sed -n '1s/^Xcode //p')"
          if [ "$actual" != "$EXPECTED_XCODE" ]; then
            echo "::error::Expected Xcode $EXPECTED_XCODE at $XCODE_APP but xcodebuild reports '$actual'."
            exit 1
          fi

      - name: Sign the disposable program ad hoc and print the DER entitlements
        run: |
          bash tool/probe_der_entitlements.sh "$RUNNER_TEMP/der-probe"

      - name: Report
        if: always()
        run: |
          {
            echo "## iOS DER entitlements diagnostics (NOT a release)"
            echo ""
            echo "This summary is written whether or not the earlier steps ran or passed. It reports no result: it does not say that signing happened,"
            echo "or that DER evidence or fixture dumps exist. Read the job log for that."
            echo ""
            echo "What the workflow is for: its probe step ad-hoc signs disposable synthetic executables (Apple's \`codesign -s -\`) and prints what"
            echo "Apple's tool wrote. It is not an application build, a production signing operation, an Apple Distribution signing operation, a release"
            echo "or an upload, and it accesses no Apple signing credential. It has no upload step and references no secret."
            echo ""
            echo "What happened is in the job log, step by step: the guard, checkout and Xcode steps must have passed before the probe step ran at all."
            echo "Only if the probe step passed, and its log ends with \`RESULT OK: DER evidence and the fixture dump were obtained for every required case\`,"
            echo "does the log hold DER evidence and the three fixture dumps (lines starting with \`RESULT\` give each case's verdict; lines starting with"
            echo "\`PROBE\` hold the details). If the probe step failed, its log shows which case and why; if it did not run, there is nothing to read."
            echo "The log of this public repository is publicly readable: only synthetic data is printed. The three fixtures are rebuilt offline with"
            echo "\`tool/extract_probe_fixtures.py\` from a saved log (docs/IOS_SIGNING.md, section 3.1b)."
            echo ""
            echo "**Success here does not prove Apple distribution compatibility.** It shows the format Apple's tool writes; Xcode makes the same"
            echo "kind of ad-hoc local call for a Distribution signature and attaches a CMS signature from Apple's cloud service, which this workflow does not exercise."
            echo ""
            echo "Xcode selected on the runner when this summary was written: $(xcodebuild -version 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
          } >> "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"
